F738 3706             (    forth6809.asm):06643         UNESCAPEW: PULU D        ; BUG FIX: was PULU D/STD UESRCLEN (storing
F73A DDAB             (    forth6809.asm):06644                    STD  UEDST     ; the popped dest-addr into the SOURCE-
                      (    forth6809.asm):06645                                   ; length variable), then LDD ,U (a PEEK, not
                      (    forth6809.asm):06646                                   ; a pop) into BOTH UEADDR and UEDST - so the
                      (    forth6809.asm):06647                                   ; real source length was misread as an
                      (    forth6809.asm):06648                                   ; address, the real source address was never
                      (    forth6809.asm):06649                                   ; popped at all, and the true destination
                      (    forth6809.asm):06650                                   ; address never reached UEDST. Correct
                      (    forth6809.asm):06651                                   ; ANS order is ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 -- ):
                      (    forth6809.asm):06652                                   ; c-addr2 (dest) is on top, popped first.
F73C 3706             (    forth6809.asm):06653                    PULU D         ; next: u1 (source length)
F73E DDA9             (    forth6809.asm):06654                    STD  UESRCLEN
F740 3706             (    forth6809.asm):06655                    PULU D         ; next: c-addr1 (source address)
F742 DDA7             (    forth6809.asm):06656                    STD  UEADDR
F744 CC0000           (    forth6809.asm):06657                    LDD  #0
F747 DDAD             (    forth6809.asm):06658                    STD  UEOUTLEN
F749 9EA7             (    forth6809.asm):06659                    LDX  UEADDR
F74B 109EAB           (    forth6809.asm):06660                    LDY  UEDST
                      (    forth6809.asm):06661         
                      (    forth6809.asm):06662                    ; ALGORITHM REPLACED (caught by MAME testing after the
                      (    forth6809.asm):06663                    ; argument-order fix above): the entire body below used
                      (    forth6809.asm):06664                    ; to decode backslash escape sequences (\n, \t, \\, \") -
                      (    forth6809.asm):06665                    ; a plausible-looking but entirely wrong reading of what
                      (    forth6809.asm):06666                    ; ANS UNESCAPE does. Confirmed against the actual spec and
                      (    forth6809.asm):06667                    ; its canonical reference implementation (forth-standard.
                      (    forth6809.asm):06668                    ; org/standard/string/UNESCAPE and complang.tuwien.ac.at's
                      (    forth6809.asm):06669                    ; ANS Forth reference text): UNESCAPE has nothing to do
                      (    forth6809.asm):06670                    ; with backslash sequences at all - it replaces every '%'
                      (    forth6809.asm):06671                    ; character with two '%' characters, and nothing else, so
                      (    forth6809.asm):06672                    ; that a literal '%' in text survives an eventual
                      (    forth6809.asm):06673                    ; SUBSTITUTE pass unchanged ("If you pass a string through
                      (    forth6809.asm):06674                    ; UNESCAPE and then SUBSTITUTE, you get the original
                      (    forth6809.asm):06675                    ; string"). Every character that ISN'T '%' passes straight
                      (    forth6809.asm):06676                    ; through, one-for-one. Confirmed via MAME: doubling a
                      (    forth6809.asm):06677                    ; single '%' wasn't happening at all, and the returned
                      (    forth6809.asm):06678                    ; count didn't grow to reflect the added characters -
                      (    forth6809.asm):06679                    ; both are direct, expected consequences of this being the
                      (    forth6809.asm):06680                    ; wrong algorithm entirely, not a smaller bug within it.
