                      (    forth6809.asm):07332         ; added this session, answering HOLDMINSIZE and PADMINSIZE
                      (    forth6809.asm):07333         ; respectively (see each entry itself, below, for why each uses its
                      (    forth6809.asm):07334         ; own, conceptually correct constant rather than the numerically-
                      (    forth6809.asm):07335         ; equal PADOFFSET).
                      (    forth6809.asm):07336         ;
                      (    forth6809.asm):07337         ; WORDLISTS (16.3.2, Search-Order word set) is deliberately absent
                      (    forth6809.asm):07338         ; from ENVTABLE, not an oversight - this system doesn't implement
                      (    forth6809.asm):07339         ; the Search-Order word set, so WORDLISTS is genuinely an unknown
                      (    forth6809.asm):07340         ; attribute here. Per ENVIRONMENT?'s own spec (6.1.1345): "If the
                      (    forth6809.asm):07341         ; system treats the attribute as unknown, the returned flag is
                      (    forth6809.asm):07342         ; false" - falling through to ENVNOTFOUND already produces exactly
                      (    forth6809.asm):07343         ; that, correctly and completely, with no dispatcher work needed.
                      (    forth6809.asm):07344         ; Confirmed via MAME testing.
                      (    forth6809.asm):07345         ;
                      (    forth6809.asm):07346         ; MAX-D/MAX-UD were resolved this session via a genuine dispatcher
                      (    forth6809.asm):07347         ; extension, not just a table entry - both are double-cell values
                      (    forth6809.asm):07348         ; per their own ANS data type, which the original single-cell
                      (    forth6809.asm):07349         ; ENVFOUND (pushing exactly one value before TRUE) couldn't
                      (    forth6809.asm):07350         ; represent. Added a second table (ENVTABLE2) and matching loop
                      (    forth6809.asm):07351         ; (ENV2START/ENV2LOOP/ENV2FOUND, below) specifically for double-cell
                      (    forth6809.asm):07352         ; entries, rather than reworking the single-cell path to handle both
